{
  "subject": {
    "slug": "research-and-evidence-based-practice",
    "title": "Research and Evidence Based Practice",
    "order": 10
  },
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "building-a-research-project",
    "title": "Building a Research Project",
    "order": 0,
    "pages": [
      {
        "title": "From Pieces to a Whole Project",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "By now you've worked through forming a question, choosing a design, defining variables, planning your analysis, and understanding ethics approval. This lesson is about assembling those pieces into one coherent, well-written research project or proposal \u2014 the kind that could actually be submitted, reviewed, and acted on."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Why the Methodology Matters",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A brilliant research question gets rejected if the methodology is flawed. The Methods section is the instruction manual for your research \u2014 it needs enough precision that another qualified professional could replicate your study without ever having to contact you for clarification."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The Purpose Statement",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Every methodology section opens by grounding the reader: a direct, formal statement of what the study intends to accomplish, followed by the specific research question. Avoid flowery language."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Example:",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " \"The purpose of this quantitative, quasi-experimental study is to determine if the implementation of a standardized pre-treatment safety checklist reduces the frequency of reported setup errors in a radiation oncology department.\""
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Defining Your Participants",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You can't simply say \"I am going to study patients.\" Define the boundaries of your population using explicit inclusion and exclusion criteria."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "tableRow",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "tableCell",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "paragraph",
                              "content": []
                            }
                          ],
                          "attrs": {
                            "header": true
                          }
                        },
                        {
